AD  - Parul University, Department of Computer Science, Waghodia, Gujarat, Vadodara, India
AB  - Object detection is a core computer vision problem that requires real-time performance as an indispensable companion of accuracy. The YOLO family (You Only Look Once) has gained popularity as it merges detection with the single-stage paradigm of detection being set side by side with speed. In this article, all developments of the YOLO model, from their seminal formulation to the current state-of-the-art in 2018-2025 publications by colleagues in areas such as aerial imagery, infrared/low illumination scenarios, underwater detection applications, agriculture and medical imaging applications, and safety/surveillance applications are critically surveyed. Instead of submitting the results in the results, performance measures such as mAP, FPS, number of parameters, and FLOPs are critically reasoned using comparative lenses. We exhibit trends such as lightweight network architecture, anchor-free detection, and preprocessing-directed variations as well as identify open challenges, including heterogeneity of the datasets, absence of reproducibility, and lack of generalization of the domain. The analysis reveals that post-2018 YOLO variants reduce computational cost by 40-70% while preserving ≥ 85% of COCO mAP, a trade-off quantified in Fig. 1. Future prospects indicate the use of the multimodal fusion, transformer-based YOLOs, federated learning for applications promoting the augmentation of privacy along with interactions using the large-scale language models. © 2025 IEEE.
