AB  - This study leverages the power of the YOLOv8 (You Only Look Once) model, a state-of-the-art object detection technology, to automatically detect and classify leaf diseases in five key crops. The primary objective is to improve both the accuracy and the efficiency of disease identification, reducing dependence on chemical pesticides, promoting environmentally friendly farming methods, and contributing to long-term agricultural sustainability. The study used a well-constructed dataset, the model training and evaluation involve 19 distinct disease categories, spanning across five major crops-rice, corn, wheat, potato, and tomato. This comprehensive dataset is utilized to enhance the model's ability to detect and classify diseases accurately. With its strength in detecting multiple objects in a single frame, YOLOv8 was employed to train a deep neural network to autonomously detect and classify leaf diseases. The model's performance was assessed through metrics like mean Average Precision (mAP) and F1 score, yielding remarkable outcomes-98% mAP and 97% F1 score. These findings highlight the potential of YOLOv8 in enhancing crop disease management and promoting sustainable agricultural practices. The findings highlight YOLOv8's potential as a powerful tool for enhancing crop disease detection, fostering more efficient agricultural operations, and supporting sustainability initiatives. Additionally, the study emphasizes the significance of using a balanced dataset and the value of data augmentation techniques in improving the robustness and generalization of disease detection models. This research underscores the potential for deploying such models on a large scale in precision agriculture, paving the way for more sustainable farming practices and improved crop health management. © 2025 IEEE.
