AD  - John P. Stevens High School, Edison, United States
AB  - Image denoising is a critical preprocessing step in biomedical imaging, as noise artifacts can reduce image quality and impair both clinical interpretation and automated analysis. Data-driven approaches have shown strong adaptability to a range of noise characteristics, with weakly supervised methods offering a practical advantage: the ability to perform denoising without requiring matched clean reference images. In this study, we assess the denoising performance of a weakly supervised learning framework, Noise2Noise, across multiple biomedical imaging modalities, including cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), ultrasound (BUS-UCLM dataset), and X-ray imaging. We additionally evaluate the pretrained Topaz-Denoise model - based on the Noise2Noise architecture - for cryo-EM data. Denoising quality was quantified using peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), structural similarity index (SSIM), signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), and contrast-to-noise ratio (CNR). Across all datasets, the weakly supervised models achieved consistent improvements in image quality and outperformed widely used conventional filters, including BM3D, non-local means (NLM), wavelet-based methods, and Gaussian blurring. To further examine model flexibility, we also investigated self-supervised approaches under data-limited conditions. The results support the effectiveness and scalability of weakly and self-supervised learning-based denoising methods for a broad range of biomedical imaging tasks, particularly where access to high-quality reference images is limited or impractical. © 2025 IEEE.
