AD  - Chongqing Three Gorges University, School of Computer Engineering and Technology, Chongqing, China
AB  - Automated detection of rice diseases and pests is critical for precision agriculture, yet existing single-stage detectors face three persistent challenges in real-world paddy field imagery: (1) lesions and pest bodies occupy only a small fraction of the image, resulting in weak and sparse feature representations; (2) conventional feature pyramid networks fuse multi-scale information with uniform channel weighting, failing to exploit the heterogeneous importance of different feature channels; (3) complex and cluttered backgrounds significantly raise the false-positive rate, undermining detection reliability under unconstrained field conditions. To address these limitations, we propose YOLO11-FEM, an enhanced single-stage detector built upon YOLO11 with three purpose-designed modules. Specifically, (1) a lightweight Feature Enhancement Module (FEM) employs multi-branch dilated convolutions to enlarge the effective receptive field and enrich local semantic representations of small targets; (2) a Feature Fusion Module (FFM) introduces a learnable channel re-weighting mechanism (CRC) that adaptively amplifies informative channels across scales; (3) the backbone is restructured with four downsampling stages to preserve fine spatial details essential for small lesion localisation. Extensive experiments on a rice disease benchmark demonstrate that YOLO11-FEM achieves superior detection accuracy compared to competitive baselines, validating the effectiveness of each proposed component through rigorous ablation analysis. © 2026 IEEE.
