AD  - School of Computer Science and Technology, Zhoukou Normal University, Henan, Zhoukou, 466001, China
AD  - Suzhou Vocational and Technological College, Anhui, Suzhou, 234000, China
AB  - Smart agriculture requires robust, real-time, and highly accurate plant disease identification systems capable of performing reliably across diverse environmental conditions. This paper presents a novel hybrid deep learning framework that integrates the You Only Look Once version 5 object detection model with a Convolutional Neural Network classification head for precise plant disease identification. The proposed pipeline begins with data acquisition using the Plant Diseases Classification benchmark dataset, followed by advanced preprocessing through the Perona-Malik Anisotropic Diffusion algorithm for noise suppression and Elastic Distortion for data augmentation. Disease-affected regions are then precisely isolated using Watershed-based gradient segmentation, after which Speeded-Up Robust Features extraction guided by Hessian-matrix keypoint detection is applied. The pipeline concludes with end-to-end classification using the proposed hybrid deep learning framework across four crop health categories: Healthy, Rust, Powdery Mildew, and Slug damage. Rigorous experimental evaluation demonstrates that the proposed model consistently achieves 98.97% accuracy, 98.86% precision, 98.88% recall, and 98.87% F1-score, outperforming state-of-the-art baselines, including EfficientNetwork, standalone You Only Look Once version 5, Vision Transformers, and standard convolutional neural network architectures, by margins of up to 5.97 percentage points over comparable deep learning baselines. Near-perfect agreement between model predictions and ground-truth labels is further supported by a Cohen's Kappa coefficient of 0.9843. The system's two-stage localisation architecture delivers a computationally efficient and scalable solution well-suited for edge deployment within Internet of Things-enabled precision agriculture systems. This work contributes a reproducible, mathematically grounded methodology alongside a comprehensive ablation study, establishing the proposed hybrid deep learning framework as a strong benchmark for automated crop health monitoring. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
