AB  - Plant disease detection through machine learning techniques has emerged as a transformative approach in agriculture, addressing the challenges of timely and accurate identification of diseases crucial for sustainable crop production. This innovative paradigm encompasses a spectrum of machine learning algorithms, from traditional (shallow) learning to deep learning. Leveraging publicly available datasets, researchers have developed models ranging from support vector machines and decision trees to advanced Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs). While traditional methods require manual feature engineering, deep learning models like CNNs automatically extract hierarchical features from raw data, eliminating this need. Despite their computational expense and data-hungry nature, researchers have mitigated these challenges through techniques like transfer learning and data augmentation. Visualization tools play a crucial role in aiding interpretation, contributing to the transparency of machine learning models. However, challenges remain, emphasizing the need for datasets collected under real cultivation conditions and addressing key issues in data collection and optimization of transfer learning processes for continued advancements in plant disease detection research. In essence, this approach promises increased efficiency and accuracy in managing diseases, contributing to the sustainable future of global agriculture. © 2025 selection and editorial matter, Sajad Un Nabi and Sheikh Mansoor; individual chapters, the contributors.
