UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105040364517&doi=10.1016%2fj.asoc.2026.115501&partnerID=40&md5=1b894eee7a3da6e0294c8bbbe1e0acaa
AD  - School of Automation, Southeast University, Nanjing, 214135, China
AD  - School of Statistics and Data Science, Southwestern University of Finance and Economics, Chengdu, 611130, China
AB  - Deep learning-based pest detection is critical for precision agriculture, yet the huge model size hinders edge deployment. Although combining knowledge distillation (KD) and network pruning (NP) reduces model size, existing sequential integration suffers from low efficiency, high time overhead, and a teacher–student “learning generation gap”. To address this, we introduce a new role, the teaching director, to measure the performance of the student model through multiple dimensions in the process of knowledge transfer, accurately control the sparsity and distillation intensity, comprehensively consider the performance of the previous two epochs and the current epoch of the student model, dynamically switch the learning modes, and realize the one-stage model compression method combining pruning and knowledge distillation. At the same time, we propose a distillation barycenter transition strategy, which gradually shifts the learning focus of the student model from the teacher model to a student-friendly assistant model in the process of knowledge distillation, so as to alleviate the problem of the “learning generation gap” between teacher and student. Validated on a self-made pest dataset (28 categories, 5678 images) and PASCAL VOC across ResNet, VGG, and CSPDarknet, the method outperforms 11 state-of-the-art methods. On ResNet, the method achieves 87.64% mAP@0.5 and 77.72% mmAP, which are 0.86% and 0.84% higher than the baseline, reducing parameters by 85.92% and FLOPs by 84.53%, enabling edge deployment for the pest detection model. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
