AB  - Effective crop management and sustainable agriculture depend on the early diagnosis of plant leaf diseases. Besides recent advancements in transformer-based models, challenges such as class imbalance, high computational complexity, and limited adaptability to diverse environmental conditions persist. This study addresses these gaps by proposing enhancements to the self-attention transformer, incorporating multi-scale feature fusion through a Feature Pyramid Network (FPN) to extract global and localized features jointly. Computational efficiency is improved by employing model pruning and quantization techniques, enabling real-time deployment on resource-constrained devices. Dynamic class-specific feature learning using contrastive loss is introduced to tackle class imbalance and improve the discrimination of visually similar disease categories. Domain adaptation strategies are also integrated to enhance the model's generalization across diverse datasets and unseen environmental conditions. When the suggested method is tested on the PlantDoc dataset, it performs better in accuracy (99.2%) and precision (70.8%). These developments support the incorporation of artificial intelligence into precision agriculture by offering a strong foundation for scalable and valuable solutions in plant disease diagnosis. © 2025 IEEE.
