AD  - Sukkur IBA University, Sukkur, 65200, Pakistan
AD  - Norwegian University of Science and Technology (NTNU), Gjøvik, 2815, Norway
AD  - Linnaeus University, Växjö, 251 95, Sweden
AB  - Real-time plant disease detection on resource-constrained edge devices requires careful trade-offs between accuracy and inference speed. Although CNNs perform well in controlled settings, deploying them on edge devices like Raspberry Pi 5 remains challenging, with limited studies comparing pruning, compression, and lightweight models. In this study, a detailed analysis of 19 CNN-based architectures (classified as small, medium, and large) is provided to detect plant diseases in real-time on a Raspberry Pi 5. We investigate the accuracy-performance trade-offs by evaluating L1-structured and L1-unstructured pruning at 50% sparsity for memory optimization of various models. Our proposed three-phase training pipeline baseline, augmented, and pruning-aware enables fine-grained analysis of pre and post-pruning performance on the PlantVillage dataset. The experimental results show that models with structured pruning outperformed those with unstructured pruning, while improving throughput from 19 models at baseline to 8 models after unstructured pruning and 13 models after structured pruning. Unstructured pruning, on the other hand, produced smaller or inconsistent frames per second (FPS) gains, reflecting limited hardware benefits from fine-grained sparsity on general-purpose CPU execution. Our benchmark quantifies accuracy-speed trade-offs across model families and facilitates practical, energy-efficient implementation of plant disease detection systems on next-generation edge devices, and offers actionable insights for researchers and practitioners in precision agriculture and Edge AI. © 2026 IEEE.
